{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological species evolve naturally; software dependencies are often chosen for efficiency/standardization, increasing correlation."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'colony' consists of clones of the same species."],"shared_structure":["Specialized organism vs. multi-species colony","Singular immune system vs. distributed ecological resilience"],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["The 'colony' (decentralized network) survives a plague (specific bug) that kills the 'organism' (centralized service), but is vulnerable to environmental shifts (infrastructure-wide outages)."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure only if its heterogeneity exceeds the 'dependency threshold' of the centralized service's redundancy.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services converge on a single industry-standard dependency (e.g., OpenSSL, as suggested in SCR_002), creating a hidden shared cause.","The cost of coordination in a decentralized network leads to consensus collapse during a crisis (SCR_004).","The centralized service implements true multi-stack heterogeneity (multi-cloud/multi-OS) that mimics decentralization (SRC_008)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Resilience is not a function of the number of nodes (redundancy) but of the diversity of failure domains. A centralized service with high redundancy often shares a single control plane or kernel (common-mode failure). A decentralized network breaks this correlation provided the services do not converge on a shared 'invisible' dependency (e.g., a specific library or cloud provider), effectively shifting the failure mode from 'systemic collapse' to 'partial degradation'.","optional_refs":["e06926d18d8d9d28bafc93900a246841ca76a8d629cf4499b036d59eb0726390","8ec632b64f425f99a2a4e088fcea1936ca7a6ed7ab3b1ce95a1d4941103aa754"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["e06926d18d8d9d28bafc93900a246841ca76a8d629cf4499b036d59eb0726390","8ec632b64f425f99a2a4e088fcea1936ca7a6ed7ab3b1ce95a1d4941103aa754"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between truly independent failures and appearing uncorrelated failures is found in the 'temporal signature' and 'dependency graph' of the outages.","counterconditions":["The 'shared cause' is so rare that it is statistically indistinguishable from independent failures over the observed timeframe.","Failures are triggered by an external adversary who targets nodes independently but sequentially."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Truly independent failures occur stochastically across the network without temporal clustering. Appearing uncorrelated failures (hidden common-mode) exhibit a 'latent trigger'—they may appear sporadic until a specific global state change (e.g., a leap second, a certificate expiration, or a BGP route leak) activates the shared vulnerability across diverse nodes simultaneously.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large service with strictly more internal redundancy is more resilient because it can implement 'global state awareness' to preempt cascading failures.","counterconditions":["The centralized control plane itself becomes the single point of failure (SRC_005).","The global rollback mechanism introduces a new, catastrophic failure mode (the 'Flash Crash' mentioned in SCR_003)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Centralized telemetry allows for the detection of early-warning signals and the execution of an instantaneous global rollback or kill-switch (SCR_003, SCR_008). A decentralized network lacks this global visibility, meaning a failure may propagate slower but becomes unstoppable once it hits a tipping point because no single entity can coordinate a network-wide mitigation.","optional_refs":["2b31792ddfabde2ffe8ecb62b06a41ea58c81225d2881c2ba87cb20071818cf9","24cc1ad82eb0aeb0550c760161210d9e8e916f55f8f70c382fe1a3b27232c38c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["2b31792ddfabde2ffe8ecb62b06a41ea58c81225d2881c2ba87cb20071818cf9","24cc1ad82eb0aeb0550c760161210d9e8e916f55f8f70c382fe1a3b27232c38c"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are fundamentally more fragile to 'thundering herd' effects than centralized services.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services implement robust, standardized circuit-breaking and back-off algorithms.","The centralized service's internal redundancy is so high that it creates its own internal thundering herd during recovery."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a decentralized system, the lack of centralized admission control means that when one small service fails, its load shifts to others without a global coordinator to throttle requests. This leads to a cascading timeout cycle (SRC_006) where the network's 'independence' actually facilitates the spread of failure through uncoordinated retry loops.","optional_refs":["24cc1ad82eb0aeb0550c760161210d9e8e916f55f8f70c382fe1a3b27232c38c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["24cc1ad82eb0aeb0550c760161210d9e8e916f55f8f70c382fe1a3b27232c38c"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["e06926d18d8d9d28bafc93900a246841ca76a8d629cf4499b036d59eb0726390","8ec632b64f425f99a2a4e088fcea1936ca7a6ed7ab3b1ce95a1d4941103aa754","2b31792ddfabde2ffe8ecb62b06a41ea58c81225d2881c2ba87cb20071818cf9","24cc1ad82eb0aeb0550c760161210d9e8e916f55f8f70c382fe1a3b27232c38c"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Could we define a 'Correlation Coefficient of Infrastructure' (CCI)? If we map every service to its dependency tree (OS, Kernel, Libs, Cloud Provider, Power Grid), the resilience of a decentralized network is inversely proportional to the overlap of these trees. If the overlap > 0 for a critical path, the network is effectively centralized regarding that failure mode.","why_keep_this":"Provides a quantitative path toward answering the 'evidence' part of the problem."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there a point where 'too much' redundancy in a centralized system actually increases the probability of correlated failure due to the complexity of the management layer?","related_refs":["SCR_005"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}